---
// Static page: built once at deploy time instead of rendered on every request.
export const prerender = true;
import '@/main.css';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Privacy &amp; Safety</title>
  </head>
  <body class="bg-background font-linux text-text-header min-h-screen">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl space-y-8 px-6 py-10 leading-relaxed">
      <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
        <img src="/a/persec.svg" alt="" class="h-8 w-8" />
        <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold">Privacy &amp; Safety</h1>
      </header>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">What Persec is</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary">
          Persec is a web proxy. Your browser connects to the Persec server, and the server fetches websites for you.
          This gets you past many website blocks, but it is not a VPN and it does not make you anonymous.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">What we collect</h2>
        <ul class="text-text-secondary list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>Persec does not keep logs of the sites you visit.</li>
          <li>
            If this Persec needs an invite code, one essential cookie keeps you signed in. It lasts 30 days from your
            last visit and does not record what you do.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your answer to the cookie notice is stored only in your own browser. You can change it in Settings → Privacy
            &amp; Cloaking.
          </li>
          <li>There are no analytics, trackers or third-party ad scripts.</li>
          <li>
            Fonts and icons are served from this server, so loading Persec does not contact Google or other companies.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your settings and bookmarks are stored only in your own browser. Clearing this site's data in your browser
            removes them.
          </li>
          <li>
            For search suggestions, words you type in the address bar are sent to DuckDuckGo by the Persec server, not
            by your browser. Web addresses you type or visit are never sent.
          </li>
          <li>The Persec server finds websites using Cloudflare's DNS, which blocks known malware sites.</li>
          <li>
            The company that hosts the server may keep its own connection logs, such as your IP address and the time you
            connected.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Saved logins (only if you turn them on)</h2>
        <ul class="text-text-secondary list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            On a Persec with invite codes, you can choose to keep a backup of the cookies and site data that sites save
            in Persec, so you are still signed in to them when you come back on any device. It does not include the
            pages you visit.
          </li>
          <li>
            The backup is encrypted in your browser with a key made from your invite code, so this server stores only
            scrambled data. Anyone who has your invite code can unlock it, including whoever runs this server (they hand
            out the codes), and anyone who signs in with the same code can restore it.
          </li>
          <li>
            Turning saved logins off, or clearing site data in Settings, deletes your backup right away. Backups that
            haven't been updated for 90 days are deleted automatically.
          </li>
          <li>If your safety is at risk, leave saved logins off.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Ads</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary">
          Ads on Persec are plain images and links. Whether a sponsor is chosen by hand or comes from an ad network,
          your browser only loads the ad's image from this server and never runs an ad script or contacts the ad
          network. If this server does use an ad network, it fetches the ad itself and tells the network nothing about
          you &mdash; the network sees only this server, not your address or browser. Clicking an ad goes through this
          server, which then sends you on to the advertiser's site, outside Persec; from there the advertiser and your
          network can see that visit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">What Persec cannot hide</h2>
        <ul class="text-text-secondary list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            Your internet provider can see that you connect to Persec, though not which pages you open through it.
          </li>
          <li>The Persec server handles your traffic, so you are trusting whoever runs it.</li>
          <li>If you log in to an account through Persec, that site knows it is you.</li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/80">
              Sites you open in Persec can read each other's cookies, including logins.
            </strong>
            Avoid logging in to important accounts through Persec. If you do log in, go to Settings → Privacy &amp;
            Cloaking → Clear site data when you're done.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">If your safety is at risk</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary">
          If you could be harmed for what you read or say online, use a tool built for that alongside or instead of
          Persec:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-text-secondary list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            <a
              class="text-[#60a5fa] underline"
              href="https://www.torproject.org/download/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noreferrer"
            >
              Tor Browser
            </a>
            hides who you are and what you visit. Where Tor is blocked, choose the Snowflake connection when it starts.
            If the Tor website is blocked, send an email to gettor@torproject.org to get a download link.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a class="text-[#60a5fa] underline" href="https://psiphon.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Psiphon
            </a>{' '}
            is a free app that gets around censorship on phones and computers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a class="text-[#60a5fa] underline" href="https://getoutline.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
              Outline
            </a>
            lets someone you trust run a private VPN server for you.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm">
        Persec is free software (AGPL-3.0), based on Lunar v2.
        <a
          class="text-[#60a5fa] underline"
          href="https://github.com/Trackertheweasel/xenapsis"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
        >
          Read the source code
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
